---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import DBox from '../../components/DBox.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '');
---

<Base
  title="Subtraction is a shipping feature — Andrew Yang"
  description="Retiring an application, two UIs and a monitoring stack without breaking anything: removal criteria, dated data clocks, estate settlement, and decisions that get un-decided."
>
  <p><a href={`${base}/lab/`}>← Lab</a></p>
  <span class="tag">operations</span>
  <h1>Subtraction is a shipping feature</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    In one recent stretch my platform retired an entire backend application, a
    container-management UI, an identity component, a monitoring stack, an edge
    proxy, and four CI runners. Nothing broke. Portfolios usually show what got
    built; this page is about the discipline of removing things — which turns
    out to have its own engineering.
  </p>

  <Diagram
    viewBox="0 0 920 194"
    alt="A dashed box across the top represents a service leaving production after its retirement criterion is met. Four arrows descend to the four parts of its estate, each settled separately: the watchers, where monitoring and drill probes are removed first so the retirement doesn't page anyone; the pipeline, where its CI deploy dies with it instead of lingering as a zombie; the data, which outlives the compute with a written removal date; and the URLs, which keep working through path-preserving redirects."
    caption="A service is more than its process. Retirement means settling the whole estate — and the order matters: un-watch before un-plug."
  >
    <DBox x={10} y={10} w={900} h={44} dashed title="a service leaves production — criterion met, not mood" />
    <DBox x={10} y={120} w={200} h={64} title="the watchers" sub="monitors and drill probes" sub2="removed first — no false page" />
    <DBox x={240} y={120} w={200} h={64} title="the pipeline" sub="its CI deploy dies with it," sub2="not as a lingering zombie" />
    <DBox x={470} y={120} w={200} h={64} title="the data" sub="outlives compute, with a" sub2="written removal date" />
    <DBox x={700} y={120} w={200} h={64} title="the URLs" sub="path-preserving redirects —" sub2="old clients keep working" />
    <path class="flow" d="M110,54 L110,114" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow" d="M340,54 L340,114" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow" d="M570,54 L570,114" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow" d="M800,54 L800,114" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <text class="lbl" x="460" y="90" text-anchor="middle">the estate, settled in order</text>
  </Diagram>

  <h2>Removal has criteria, not moods</h2>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li>
      <strong>An application:</strong> retired after the stated criterion was
      met — zero real traffic for seven days, measured, not assumed. Its data
      stays untouched in the shared database; deletion of data and deletion of
      compute are separate decisions on separate clocks.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A management UI:</strong> retired when its benefit fell below its
      cost — with the cost stated honestly: the tool's admin token was
      root-equivalent for the whole machine, an attack surface purchased for
      the convenience of viewing twelve containers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An identity component:</strong> removed because it was the only
      permanently-red monitoring target. A red light that never means anything
      trains the operator to ignore red lights.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The mechanics of a clean retirement</h2>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li>
      <strong>Un-watch before un-plug:</strong> remove the monitoring that
      points at a service before killing the service, or the retirement itself
      pages you. Then remove the probes from the cold-boot checklist — a drill
      that fails on ghosts stops being trusted.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Data outlives compute on a dated clock:</strong> volumes and
      tables survive their services with an explicit removal date written down,
      so "keep it just in case" cannot quietly become forever.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Paths get forwarding addresses:</strong> retired URLs return
      path-preserving redirects; API clients and old bookmarks keep working
      while the system underneath is replaced.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Hunt the zombies:</strong> the sneakiest leftovers are deploy
      pipelines — I found two CI workflows still trying to deploy services that
      had moved runtimes weeks earlier. A service's pipeline, monitoring,
      checklist entries and docs form its estate, and the estate must be
      settled.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The other direction: un-deciding</h2>
  <p>
    My TODO file has a section called "deliberately not doing — with reasons."
    The test of that section is not that it exists; it is that entries
    <em>leave</em> it when their reasons die. Recent examples, each recorded
    with what changed:
  </p>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li>
      "Backend secrets stay in CI secrets" was reversed when I noticed the
      stated reason — <em>the truth already lives elsewhere</em> — had become
      the problem: the main application's credentials were the only secrets
      outside the encrypted-in-git system, taking a detour through a CI
      runner and a plaintext file on every deploy.
    </li>
    <li>
      A database ownership table I wrote confidently was corrected days later
      when evidence contradicted it. The table's value survived the error —
      because a wrong entry in a maintained registry gets fixed, while wrong
      folklore just spreads.
    </li>
    <li>
      A database migration shipped with its <em>reversal</em> documented as a
      first-class artifact — observation window, rollback path, and the date
      the rollback path itself expires.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What I'd tell my past self</h2>
  <p>
    Measure your system by what it no longer contains. Every retired component
    is maintenance you stopped paying, attack surface you stopped defending,
    and — most valuable — one less thing whose failure modes you have to hold
    in your head. Addition is easy to celebrate; subtraction is where the
    judgment shows.
  </p>
</Base>
